Perform a thorough static security code review of this WordPress plugin source code to identify real security weaknesses, prioritizing unauthenticated and low-privilege issues such as SQL injection, XSS, command execution, path traversal, arbitrary file upload or deletion, SSRF, broken access control, privilege escalation, and CSRF. Start by mapping all external entry points, including wp_ajax_*, wp_ajax_nopriv_*, register_rest_route, admin_init, shortcodes, and HTTP inputs like $_GET, $_POST, $_REQUEST, $_FILES, and php://input, along with any Semgrep findings, then read the actual PHP and JS files to trace full source-to-sink data flows. Discard any path properly protected by sanitization, prepared statements, escaping, nonces such as wp_verify_nonce or check_ajax_referer, or capability checks such as current_user_can. Report only verified findings with exact file and line citations, required privilege level, source-to-sink data flow trace, reproduction conditions for verification, and recommended WordPress remediation.
